{
  "id": "bridging-amoebots",
  "model": "programmable-matter",
  "name": "Bridging",
  "origin": {
    "ref": "AndresArroyo2018Bridging",
    "year": 2017,
    "note": "arXiv 2017; Natural Computing 2018."
  },
  "derived_from": {
    "problem": "compression-amoebots",
    "kind": "generalisation of",
    "relation": "Bridging adds terrain: the compression chain is biased to stay on land and to shorten a path across a gap [AndresArroyo2018Bridging]."
  },
  "summary": "Particles moving between two sites must build a bridge across a gap that balances path length against bridge cost.",
  "statement": "Particles occupy a path between two fixed sites across a gap in the terrain. They run a local Markov chain that rewards neighbours and penalises particles over the gap. The system should form a shortcut bridge whose length and cost are balanced, with high probability in the stationary distribution.",
  "primer": {
    "intro": "Army ants build living bridges out of their own bodies to cut a corner across a gap: a longer bridge saves walking but takes more ants out of work. Here particles on the triangular grid form a connected group joining two fixed sites, and some nodes are marked as *gap* (over the hole) and others as *land*; a particle can tell which kind of node it is on. A particle sits on one node of an endless triangular grid and moves in two steps: it *expands* into an empty neighbouring node, briefly occupying both, then *contracts* into one of the two; so one expansion and one contraction move it by one node. One randomly chosen particle acts at a time, with constant memory and no compass. The group should settle into a bridge that balances a short path between the two sites against few particles hanging over the gap, measured by a weighted perimeter where boundary edges over the gap cost more.",
    "ideas": [
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "The compression rule, with the gap made expensive",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "AndresArroyo2018Bridging",
        "idea": "There are two biases: $\\lambda > 1$ rewards a short perimeter (many neighbours), and a second bias, written $\\gamma$ in [AndresArroyo2018Bridging], rewards a short perimeter over the gap.\\n\\n1. A randomly chosen particle picks a random empty neighbouring node.\\n2. It checks locally that the move neither splits the group nor encloses a hole.\\n3. It works out, from its neighbourhood, how the move changes the perimeter and the part of the perimeter that lies over the gap.\\n4. It moves with a probability that is larger when both get shorter (certainly, when the move improves both).",
        "analysis": "For $\\lambda > 2+\\sqrt2 \\approx 3.41$ and $\\gamma > 1$, the weighted perimeter is within a constant factor of its minimum with all but exponentially small probability [AndresArroyo2018Bridging]. For a V-shaped gap the bridge settles at a position that depends on the angle of the V, as observed in real ant bridges. The threshold $2+\\sqrt2$ is the same as for compression, and for the same counting reason; for small biases the paper gives no guarantee.",
        "why_it_matters": "It showed that the compression rule is a flexible tool: changing the weights of boundary edges changes the shape the swarm settles into."
      },
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Why only one bridge forms",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "Oh2024Bridge",
        "idea": "Fire ants searching for food across water start several bridges but end with only one. [Oh2024Bridge] model this with particles that prefer to move in one direction (towards the food) and prefer having neighbours, with one bias for each preference.\\n\\n1. Particles move at random, but a step towards the food is more likely than a step away.\\n2. A step that gains neighbours is more likely than one that loses them.\\n3. Nothing else: no particle knows how many bridges exist.",
        "analysis": "When both biases are large, a single bridge forms almost certainly [Oh2024Bridge]. Intuitively, two bridges have more boundary than one bridge made of the same particles, and the neighbour bias makes long boundaries unlikely. The proof follows which nodes are occupied rather than which particle is where, which simplifies the analysis.",
        "why_it_matters": "It shows that a striking collective decision, choosing one bridge, can arise from two simple preferences without coordination."
      }
    ]
  },
  "history": "Bridging was inspired by army ants, whose bridges depend on the gap angle [AndresArroyo2018Bridging]; the later question was whether a **single** bridge forms [Oh2024Bridge].",
  "variants": [
    {
      "id": "stochastic",
      "title": "Markov chain, sequential",
